--TEST--
A `catch` arm naming `Core\Script\Finished` is refused — a finish is an ending, not a failure a request recovers from (`rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue`, `rule:concurrency/after-response-outlives-the-connection`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The class `Core\Script::finish()` raises is a root of its own rather than
// a `Throwable`, so an arm naming it would be false against every value that
// ever reaches one. It is refused where it is written instead of matching
// nothing quietly: the code a site wants to run on the way out belongs in a
// `finally`, which the unwind runs, or in a `Core\Script::onExit` hook,
// which the ending fires.
try {
    echo "body\n";
} catch (Core\Script\Finished $done) {
    echo "never\n";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0814]: `Core\Script\Finished` is not a class a `catch` can name
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
